# EXAMPLE ONLY — DO NOT PASTE BLINDLY.
#
# Goal:
#   Linux source 192.168.88.201 -> existing LTE1 routing table
#   Linux source 192.168.88.202 -> existing LTE2 routing table
#
# OpenClaw MUST first inspect:
#   /routing/table/print detail
#   /ip/route/print detail
#   /routing/rule/print detail
#   /ip/firewall/mangle/print detail
#   /ip/firewall/nat/print detail
#
# Prefer reusing the routing tables already used by the ELMO port-based rules.
# Back up first:
#   /export file=before-ltap-test-routing
#
# If the existing configuration uses mangle routing marks, two temporary rules can
# normally be inserted BEFORE the existing generic/port rules.
#
# Replace <TABLE_FOR_LTE1> and <TABLE_FOR_LTE2> with VERIFIED existing table names.
#
# /ip/firewall/mangle
# add chain=prerouting src-address=192.168.88.201/32 action=mark-routing \
#     new-routing-mark=<TABLE_FOR_LTE1> passthrough=no comment="TEMP LTAP TEST LTE1"
# add chain=prerouting src-address=192.168.88.202/32 action=mark-routing \
#     new-routing-mark=<TABLE_FOR_LTE2> passthrough=no comment="TEMP LTAP TEST LTE2"
#
# Ensure existing srcnat/masquerade rules cover both LTE egress paths.
#
# IMPORTANT:
# - Do not create new default routes until the existing routing logic is understood.
# - Do not reorder production ELMO rules except the minimum needed for the two
#   temporary source-IP test rules.
# - After setup, prove the path using LTE interface byte counters while sending
#   traffic bound to .201 and .202.
# - The collector also performs a direction-aware path verification and flags
#   tests where the non-selected LTE interface carried substantial traffic.
